    ; Drop the whole event queue and restart the numbering — the streaming
    ; counterpart of reset_event_scratch, called at the same seam.
    ;
    ; A drain consumes [drained, n_events()) COMPLETELY and then frees the
    ; events themselves with the scratch region, so at that point every entry in
    ; the queue dangles: the queue is dead in full, not just below a cursor.
    ; Keeping it only cost memory — 8 bytes per event of the whole document,
    ; measured 33.1 MB of capacity (17.5 MB of it actually written) on a 13.7 MB
    ; parse, next to 248 MB of run region. A fresh Array grows from small again,
    ; so its own `reallocate` releases each exclusive page it outgrows and only
    ; the ≤1 KB bump buffers are abandoned per drain.
    ;
    ; ★Every consumer reads the live window as [get_drained(), n_events())
    ; (Esis.write, the dazzle grove sink), so restarting both at 0 is
    ; transparent to them. The consumers that walk the WHOLE queue — RAST,
    ; the ArcEngine, -p — are exactly the modes where streaming stays off, so
    ; this never runs for them, the same gate reset_event_scratch already has.
    procedure clear_events(mutable this: ref[ParserState]) linked





